## Context

Ticket 0026 was ruled a monster on 2026-09-02 and decomposed by ladder rung;
this is rung 4 of five children, and 0026 is the tracker. Goal 4 is "it finds the
right thing, in my languages, and I can open it" (README.md, "## The goals
ladder"), and the golden gate is the instrument that decides R34 — every pinned
answer comes back within the first ten results.

The gate has two readings of one pinned set, and they are opposite on purpose.
The stability reading compares a run against the last and tolerates legitimate
drift, which is what SPEC.md §5.2.8's thresholds are for; the R34 reading
compares the run against the pinned answers and tolerates none. A corpus that
can be stable and wrong is exactly why both exist. Order is deliberately
ungated, and §5.2.8 records why: an order gate flakes, gets turned off, and that
is how a past defect happened.

This ticket waits on ticket 0029, which delivers the pinned multilingual fixture
corpus and the Zotero-free index harness. There is nothing to gate until the set
is pinned, and the set is not this ticket's to invent.

## Relevant files

- `SPEC.md` §5.2.8, "**The golden gate (D11 = set)**" — the thresholds, the
  faceting rule, the cross-lingual slice, and the two readings. Every number the
  gate asserts is that section's; cite the address, never the digit.
- `tickets/0029-the-golden-fixture-corpus-and-a-zotero-f.erg` — the pinned set
  and the harness this gate runs over.
- `README.md`, "## Goal 4 — it finds the right thing, in my languages, and I can
  open it" — the rung's roster; R34 is this ticket's, the other five are ticket
  0029's.
- `bench/results/` — where a re-pin's set diff lands as the review artifact.

## Actions

1. The gate itself, over ticket 0029's pinned set at k=10, computing answer sets
   rather than orders, and asserting SPEC.md §5.2.8's three thresholds by
   reference.
2. The R34 reading beside the stability reading, tolerating no drift, so a
   regression names which of the two it broke.
3. The cross-lingual slice gated separately from the monolingual queries, so a
   regression names which of R7 and R29 it broke.
4. Facet-aware rung evaluation: each pinned query records at pinning which corpus
   its answer needs, and this rung binds the queries whose facet the corpus
   already covers. The rest join goal 5's evaluation when their corpus lands, and
   the gate says which subset it evaluated rather than reporting a bare pass.
5. Entry granularity when entries exist; until then the gate covers item
   projections and says so in its own output.
6. A re-pin is a commit whose set diff is the review artifact. Make that the
   mechanism, not a convention someone has to remember.

## Test

Red first, and the failure class is already measured: §5.2.8 records the
observed legitimate per-query Jaccard minimum and the failure class's own
measured value, which is far below it. Build a fixture that reproduces the
failure class and see the gate go red on it before any green run — a gate whose
floor was never crossed has not been shown to have a floor.

The second red arm is the one the two readings exist for: perturb the index
legitimately, and require the stability reading to pass while the R34 reading
fails on a moved pinned answer. If both readings agree on every input, one of
them is not being computed.

## Verification

- [ ] The gate ran red against a fixture reproducing the failure class, before
      any green run.
- [ ] Stability and R34 readings disagree on the constructed input that should
      separate them.
- [ ] The cross-lingual slice reports separately from the monolingual queries.
- [ ] The gate names the facet subset it evaluated.
- [ ] No threshold is written in this repository outside SPEC.md §5.2.8.
- [ ] `make check` green; `./tickets/erg check` green.

## Invariants

- The unit is the MUST clause, not the requirement: one assertion per clause, so
  README.md's `delivered` column becomes a count rather than a reader's
  judgement. Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026.
- Honest red and waiver states in the Makefile, never a green that means "could
  not look". Inherited from the tracker, ticket 0026. A gate that finds no pinned
  set must say so, not pass.
- Order stays ungated. Re-introducing an order assertion needs a ruling in
  `DECISIONS.md`, not a commit here.
- Thresholds live once, in SPEC.md §5.2.8. A number quoted here is the second
  copy that drifts.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] the golden gate runs over ticket 0029's pinned set at k=10, on answer sets
- [ ] SPEC.md §5.2.8's three thresholds asserted by reference, none copied
- [ ] the R34 reading asserted beside the stability reading, tolerating no drift
- [ ] the cross-lingual slice gated separately from the monolingual queries
- [ ] rung evaluation bound to the covered facet subset, and the subset named
- [ ] the gate seen red against a fixture reproducing the measured failure class
- [ ] in the Makefile with honest red/waiver states
